WEBVTT

00:00.428 --> 00:01.795
- [Voiceover] Let's say that we have

00:01.795 --> 00:05.536
the differential equation
dy dx or the derivative

00:05.536 --> 00:10.536
of y with respect to x is
equal to negative x over y.

00:11.519 --> 00:14.844
Let's say we don't know how
to find the solutions to this,

00:14.844 --> 00:16.845
but we at least want to get a sense

00:16.944 --> 00:19.012
of what the solutions might look like.

00:19.441 --> 00:22.337
And to do that what we
could do is we could look

00:22.337 --> 00:26.811
at a coordinate plane, so
let me draw some axes here.

00:27.305 --> 00:31.171
So let me draw relatively
straight line alright.

00:31.459 --> 00:36.288
So that's my y-axis and this is my x-axis.

00:38.826 --> 00:43.820
Let me mark this as one, that's
two, that's negative one,

00:43.082 --> 00:48.082
negative two, one, two,
negative one, and negative two.

00:51.304 --> 00:54.210
And what I could do is since
this differential equation

00:54.021 --> 00:57.962
is just in terms of xs and
ys and first derivatives

00:58.151 --> 01:02.476
of y with respect to x, I
could sample points on the

01:02.476 --> 01:05.382
coordinate plane, I could look
at the x and y coordinates

01:05.382 --> 01:07.079
substitute them in here, figure out

01:07.079 --> 01:08.686
what the slope is going to be.

01:08.686 --> 01:11.323
And then I could visualize
the slope if a solution

01:11.323 --> 01:14.161
goes through that point what
the slope needs to be there

01:14.161 --> 01:16.176
and I can visualize that
with a line segment,

01:16.176 --> 01:17.642
a little small line segment that has

01:17.642 --> 01:20.364
the same slope as the slope in question.

01:20.784 --> 01:22.745
So let's actually do that.

01:23.175 --> 01:24.926
So let me setup a little table here.

01:25.978 --> 01:27.083
I'm going to do a little table here

01:27.083 --> 01:29.110
to do a bunch of x and y values.

01:29.011 --> 01:30.455
Once again I'm just sampling some points

01:30.554 --> 01:32.647
on the coordinate plane
to be able to visualize.

01:33.133 --> 01:38.133
So x, y and this is dy dx.

01:38.983 --> 01:43.983
So let's say when x is zero and y is one,

01:44.979 --> 01:47.137
what is the derivative
of y with respect to x?

01:47.453 --> 01:49.017
It's going to be negative zero over one

01:49.017 --> 01:50.814
so it's just going to be zero.

01:51.387 --> 01:54.543
And so at the point zero one
if a solution goes through

01:54.543 --> 01:57.740
this point its slope is going to be zero.

01:57.977 --> 02:00.480
And so we can visualize
that by doing a little

02:00.048 --> 02:02.317
horizontal line segment right there.

02:03.159 --> 02:04.412
So let's keep going.

02:04.697 --> 02:08.860
What about when x is one and y is one?

02:09.634 --> 02:12.138
Well then dy dx, the derivative
of y with respect to x

02:12.138 --> 02:15.390
is negative one over one, so
it's going to be negative one.

02:15.781 --> 02:19.377
So at the point one comma
one if a solution goes

02:19.377 --> 02:24.377
through that point, it would
have a slope of negative one.

02:26.236 --> 02:27.558
And so I draw a little line segment

02:27.558 --> 02:29.436
that has a slope of negative one.

02:31.358 --> 02:33.071
Let me do this in a new color.

02:33.071 --> 02:36.693
What about when x is one and y is zero?

02:37.041 --> 02:40.656
Well then it's negative one over zero,

02:41.025 --> 02:44.726
so this is actually
undefined, but it's a clue

02:44.726 --> 02:49.501
that maybe the slope there
if you had a tangent line

02:49.501 --> 02:51.472
at that point, maybe it's vertical.

02:51.472 --> 02:56.472
So I'll put that as a
question mark, vertical there.

02:56.887 --> 02:59.936
So maybe it's something
like that if you actually

03:00.377 --> 03:02.378
did have, I guess it
wouldn't be a function,

03:02.477 --> 03:05.208
if you had some kind of
relation that went through it,

03:05.208 --> 03:08.419
but let's not draw that just yet.

03:08.419 --> 03:09.815
But let's try some other points.

03:12.728 --> 03:16.434
Let's try the point
negative one negative one.

03:17.303 --> 03:20.325
So now we have negative negative one,

03:20.325 --> 03:21.927
which is one, over negative one.

03:21.927 --> 03:24.245
Well you'd have a slope
of negative one here.

03:24.245 --> 03:25.970
So negative one negative one

03:25.097 --> 03:30.097
you'd have a slope of negative one.

03:31.896 --> 03:35.620
What about if you had one negative one?

03:36.279 --> 03:37.670
Well now it's negative one over

03:37.067 --> 03:39.278
negative one, your slope is now one.

03:39.881 --> 03:43.418
So one negative one, if a
solution goes through this

03:43.418 --> 03:46.097
its slope would look like that.

03:46.049 --> 03:49.024
And we could keep going, we
could even do two negative two.

03:49.465 --> 03:52.586
That's going to have a
slope of one as well.

03:52.978 --> 03:56.937
If you did positive two positive two,

03:56.937 --> 03:58.262
that would be negative two over two.

03:58.262 --> 04:00.790
You'd have a slope of
negative one right over here.

04:01.181 --> 04:05.230
And so we could do a bunch
of points, just keep going.

04:05.023 --> 04:06.855
Now I'm just doing them in my head,

04:07.062 --> 04:08.284
I'm not going on the table.

04:08.284 --> 04:10.272
But you get a sense of
what's going on here.

04:10.272 --> 04:13.177
Here you're slope, what if
it was negative one one?

04:13.177 --> 04:16.161
It's going to have a slope of one.

04:16.022 --> 04:19.079
So at this point your
slope negative one one

04:19.079 --> 04:21.236
so negative negative one is one over one,

04:21.947 --> 04:23.464
so you're going to have a slope like that.

04:23.464 --> 04:26.332
At negative two two same exact idea,

04:26.332 --> 04:27.505
it would look like that.

04:27.505 --> 04:30.268
And so when you keep
drawing these line segments

04:30.268 --> 04:33.351
over these kind of sample
points in the cartesian

04:33.351 --> 04:37.023
or in the x-y plane, you
start to get a sense of

04:37.023 --> 04:39.770
well what would a solution have to do.

04:40.127 --> 04:43.443
And you can start to visualize
that hey maybe a solution

04:44.464 --> 04:49.464
would have to do something like this.

04:49.501 --> 04:52.820
This would be a solution,
so maybe it would

04:52.082 --> 04:53.731
have to do something like this.

04:54.469 --> 04:58.861
Or if we're looking only at
functions and not relations

04:58.861 --> 05:02.276
I'll make it so it's very clear.

05:02.691 --> 05:04.562
So maybe it would have to
do something like this.

05:04.562 --> 05:09.006
Or if the function started
here based on what we've

05:09.006 --> 05:14.006
seen so far maybe it would
have to do something like this.

05:15.424 --> 05:20.212
Or if this were a point
on the function over here,

05:20.212 --> 05:23.524
it would have to do something like this.

05:23.524 --> 05:25.020
And once again I'm doing this based on

05:25.002 --> 05:27.336
what the slope field is telling me.

05:28.246 --> 05:31.430
So this field that I'm
creating where I'm sampling

05:31.043 --> 05:32.531
a bunch of points and I'm visualizing

05:32.918 --> 05:34.635
the slope with a line segement.

05:34.635 --> 05:39.635
Once again this is called a slope field.

05:42.805 --> 05:43.996
So hopefully that gives you kind of

05:43.996 --> 05:45.894
the basic idea of what a slope field is.

05:45.894 --> 05:50.127
And the next two videos we'll
explore this idea even deeper.
